course: dictionary
lesson: 13797
title: Inverted
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Inverted means turned upside down, or the other way round. Think of a cup that has been turned over on the table: it is inverted. Context helps you check this. «The empty cup was inverted on the shelf» tells you the cup was upside down, not just lying there.
explain: In English you will meet inverted commas around speech. They are the marks ‘ ’ or « » that show exactly what someone said. «‘Come here,’ said Mum.» The marks go around Mum's words, and «said Mum» tells us who spoke. These marks are also called speech marks or quotation marks.
explain: The same word works in maths and science. An inverted fraction is turned over, so 2/3 becomes 3/2. A lens can make an inverted image, which is a picture that appears upside down. When you meet inverted, ask: what is being turned over or turned round?
fact: Inverted means turned upside down or the other way round.
fact: Inverted commas are the marks ‘ ’ or « » used around speech or a quote.
fact: Inverted commas are also called speech marks or quotation marks.
fact: In maths, an inverted fraction is turned over: 2/3 becomes 3/2.
fact: In science, a lens can make an inverted image, which is an upside-down picture.
exercise: «The empty cup was inverted on the shelf.» What does inverted mean here? | *Turned upside down | Broken into pieces | Full of juice to the top | Very shiny and new
exercise: «‘Come here,’ said Mum.» Which marks go around the words Mum actually said? | *Inverted commas | Colons | Hyphens | Brackets
exercise: In maths, 2/3 becomes 3/2 when it is inverted. What has happened to the fraction? | It has been rounded up | It has been doubled | *It has been turned over | It has been hidden away
test: A lens makes a picture of a tree that is upside down. What is this picture called? | Blurry | *Inverted | Enlarged | Coloured
test: Another name for inverted commas is what? | *Speech marks | Full stops | Hyphens | Bullet points
exam: Which sentence uses inverted commas correctly? | ‘Hello’, said Ali, | Hello,’ said Ali. | ‘Hello, said Ali. | *‘Hello,’ said Ali.
